## 3D Text in the Viewer — Use `label-sprite-factory.ts`

Any text rendered inside the 3D viewer (labels on sound spheres, receivers, bounding-box
faces, dimension readouts, etc.) must go through `frontend/src/lib/three/label-sprite-factory.ts`:

```typescript
import { createLabelSprite, updateLabelSprite, disposeLabelSprite } from '@/lib/three/label-sprite-factory'

// Create (canvas + texture + SpriteMaterial, layers 0 & 4, renderOrder 1001)
const sprite = createLabelSprite('Right')
sprite.position.set(x, y, z)
scene.add(sprite)

// Update text IN PLACE (same canvas, no sprite recreation → no scale flash)
updateLabelSprite(sprite, '6.2 m')

// Dispose GPU resources when done
disposeLabelSprite(sprite)
```

**Never** hand-roll `document.createElement('canvas')` + `CanvasTexture` + `SpriteMaterial`
for viewer text (BoundingBoxManager previously did this — see git history).

Sizing rules:

1. `createLabelSprite` does **not** set a world scale — the caller owns `sprite.scale`.
2. Use screen-space sizing so text keeps a constant apparent size at any zoom:
   `h = 2 · distance · tan(fov/2) · OBJECT_LABEL.VIEWPORT_HEIGHT_RATIO`, then
   `sprite.scale.set(h · userData.aspectRatio, h, 1)`. See `updateScreenSpaceScale()` in
   `sound-sphere-manager.ts` / `receiver-manager.ts` / `BoundingBoxManager.ts` for the pattern.
3. Font/padding/radius constants come from `OBJECT_LABEL` in `utils/constants.ts`.
4. Call `updateLabelSprite` (not a new sprite) when only the text changes — recreating
   flashes the label at the default (huge) sprite scale for a frame.

